TI  - An open-label, single-arm study of CRYO2 for tissue removal at the site of 
      central airway obstruction or stenosis: study protocol.
PG  - 411-415
LID - 10.18999/nagjms.80.3.411 [doi]
AB  - Argon-plasma coagulation, high-frequency electrosurgical snare, laser therapy, 
      and microwave treatment are therapeutic options to reduce tumor-related stenosis 
      of the central airway. These treatments may cause airway fire under a high 
      concentration of oxygen, so FiO2 levels must be ≤40%. This restriction may be 
      dangerous when treating patients with respiratory failure. The cryosurgery unit, 
      ERBE-CRYO2, facilitates treatment under an FiO(2) level of 100%, safely reducing 
      this risk. In Japan, CRYO2 has been approved for cryobiopsy and foreign body 
      removal, but not for tissue removal at the site of obstruction or stenosis due to 
      lack of sufficient evidence. Since CRYO2 may be useful for reducing airway 
      stenosis, the present study was designed to increase indications for this unit in 
      Japan.
