VI  - 89
IP  - 3
DP  - 2021
TI  - A tracheobronchial adenoid cystic carcinoma incidentally detected by chest 
      radiography and pulmonary function test.
PG  - 320-323
LID - 10.5603/ARM.a2021.0003 [doi]
AB  - Tracheobronchial adenoid cystic carcinomas are rare tumors that progress 
      slowly.　Diagnosis by chest plain radiography is difficult, and suspecting the 
      existence of a disease that causes stenosis in the airways is an important key 
      for diagnosis. A 51-year-old woman referred to our hospital because of dyspnea on 
      exertion. Chest plain radiograph showed a slight widening of the mediastinum. The 
      flow-volume curve of respiratory function test revealed flat portions of the 
      curve suggesting the central airway obstruction pattern. Chest CT confirmed the 
      existence of tracheobronchial tumor, which was pathologically diagnosed as 
      adenoid cystic carcinoma. Chest physicians and thoracic surgeons should be 
      careful not to miss these trivial findings in the first inspections.
