TI  - A Rare Case of Subglottic Rosai-Dorfman-Destombes Disease Causing Fixed 
      Upper-Airway Obstruction in an Adult Woman.
PG  - 006700
LID - 10.12890/2026_006700 [doi]
LID - 006700
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Rosai-Dorfman-Destombes disease (RDD) is a rare non-Langerhans cell 
      histiocytosis that usually presents with painless cervical lymphadenopathy but 
      may involve extranodal sites. Laryngeal or subglottic involvement is uncommon yet 
      clinically significant because progressive fixed upper airway obstruction may 
      initially resemble more common obstructive airway disease. CASE DESCRIPTION: We 
      report a 59-year-old lifelong non-smoker with a history of mediastinal 
      lymphadenopathy who developed several months of progressive exertional dyspnoea 
      and wheeze treated as moderate persistent asthma with escalating inhaled therapy 
      and repeated systemic steroids, without sustained improvement. Pulmonary 
      evaluation demonstrated only mild obstructive physiology with air trapping and 
      reduced diffusing capacity, discordant with the patient's marked exertional 
      limitation. On follow-up, she was noted to have audible biphasic stridor, 
      prompting urgent evaluation for central airway obstruction. Flexible laryngoscopy 
      revealed an exophytic subglottic mass causing approximately 50% fixed airway 
      obstruction. She underwent tracheostomy for airway protection followed by 
      excision of the lesion. Histology demonstrated a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate 
      with large S100 and CD68 positive histiocytes showing emperipolesis, consistent 
      with extranodal RDD. Cross sectional imaging excluded multifocal disease. She was 
      decannulated and remains asymptomatic under surveillance. CONCLUSION: This case 
      underscores that fixed central airway obstruction can masquerade as refractory 
      asthma and that biphasic stridor may be the critical bedside clue prompting 
      reconsideration of the diagnosis. Although subglottic RDD is rare, delayed 
      recognition may lead to life-threatening airway compromise. Compared with the 
      usual nodal presentation and often indolent clinical course of RDD, isolated 
      subglottic disease is unusual and clinically significant because diagnosis 
      depends on biopsy of a structural airway lesion and localized disease may have a 
      favourable prognosis after endoscopic treatment and surveillance. LEARNING 
      POINTS: Progressive wheeze and dyspnoea with limited response to 
      guideline-directed asthma therapy, particularly when symptom severity is 
      disproportionate to spirometry, should prompt evaluation for non-asthma causes of 
      airflow obstruction.Biphasic stridor is a key red flag for fixed central airway 
      obstruction and warrants urgent airway visualization.Localized subglottic 
      Rosai-Dorfman-Destombes disease, although rare, can be managed effectively with 
      surgical debulking and temporary airway protection; systemic therapy is reserved 
      for multifocal or organ threatening disease.
